# Runs page: Build and Analytics histories become tabs

## Context

The Runs page shipped earlier today with the two histories stacked — Build
runs above, Analytics runs below, both always rendered. With eleven columns
on the analytics table the page had grown tall enough that reaching the
second table meant scrolling past the whole of the first. Tabs put both one
click away and match the tab convention already used on the Analytics page.

This is a layout change only. No element id moved, so the JavaScript
contract with `domain-runs.js` is untouched and no loader logic changed.

## Changes

1. `src/front/templates/partials/domain/_domain_runs.html` — replaced the two
   stacked `<h6>`-headed blocks with a `nav nav-tabs ob-tabs` strip and a
   `tab-content ob-tab-content` body, following the same house convention as
   the Analytics page tabs. Two panes: `rtab-build` (default, `show active`)
   and `rtab-analytics`. Every existing element id is preserved verbatim.
   Added `aria-controls` / `aria-selected` on the buttons and
   `aria-labelledby` on the panes.
2. Same file — the analytics pane's loading and empty states moved from
   `py-4` to `py-5`, matching the build pane. As sibling tabs showing the
   same kind of state, differing padding was visible when switching between
   them; the two blocks were only inconsistent because one predated the
   other.
3. Same file — the two error alerts gained `m-3`. Inside a bordered
   `ob-tab-content` body an alert would otherwise sit flush against the
   pane's edge.
4. `tests/units/front/test_runs_page.py` — added `TestRunsTabs` (5 tests):
   both tabs and panes render; each button targets its own pane; exactly one
   tab is active and it is Build runs; each pane is labelled by its tab; and
   each table sits inside its own pane. Renamed
   `test_both_cards_have_their_own_elements` to `..._tabs_...` and updated
   the module docstring.
5. `docs/user-guide.md` — the Runs section now describes tabs rather than two
   stacked tables, notes Build runs is the tab shown first, and states that
   both panes are populated on section entry so switching never waits on a
   fetch.

## Design notes

- **Both tabs load on section entry**, rather than lazy-loading the analytics
  table when its tab is first opened. The two loaders are already independent
  and `_runsLoaded` already latches per-section-entry, so lazy loading would
  have added a second caching concern for no benefit. Switching tabs is
  instant.
- **The Refresh button stays above the tab strip and reloads both.** It is a
  property of the section, not of the visible tab.
- **Build runs is the default** — it was the upper table before, and it is
  the history that exists for every domain, whereas analytics runs only
  appear once someone has run an analysis.

