# Analytics Delta tables surfaced in the Settings → Objects tabs

## Context

Every Run Analysis writes Unity Catalog Delta tables through the Lakeflow job:
`graph_metrics_<slug>` plus `_summary`, `_type_profiles`, `_type_predicates`,
and — when a run dies before its own cleanup — stranded `_work_<stage>` scratch
tables. `DigitalTwin.analytics_output_table` places them in
`analytics_job_output_schema`, defaulting to the registry `catalog.schema`, so
they sit right next to the triple-store objects.

Neither Objects tab could see them. Settings → Lakehouse → Objects listed only
names starting with `triplestore_`; Settings → Lakebase → Objects listed
Postgres objects plus the Lakeflow sync sub-block. An operator deleting a
domain version therefore dropped the graph and silently left the analytics
tables behind, with no UI able to find them afterwards.

Design: `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-02-objects-tab-analytics-tables-design.md`.

## Changes

1. `src/back/core/graphdb/delta/objects.py` — Added the analytics grouping
   helpers alongside the existing triple-store ones: `analytics_base` (strips
   `_work_<stage>` then a companion suffix), `analytics_match_key` (the
   `<safe>_<version>` slug), `domain_match_key` (`triplestore_<safe>_V<n>` →
   `<safe>_<n>`), `_analytics_drop_sort_key`, and `group_analytics_objects`,
   which returns the same `{key, base, items, sorted_items}` shape as
   `group_triplestore_objects`.
2. `src/back/objects/domain/SettingsService.py` —
   `triple_store_databricks_objects_result` now also returns `analytics`,
   `orphans`, `analytics_location` and `analytics_message`, and each `domains`
   entry carries its `key` so the front end never re-parses names. The new
   private `_analytics_objects` resolves the analytics schema the same way
   `DigitalTwin._build_job_metrics` does — `analytics_job_output_schema` when
   set, the registry schema otherwise — reusing the caller's enumeration when
   the two coincide and scanning separately when they do not. A failed scan
   returns its reason instead of raising, so the triple-store listing still
   renders.
3. `src/front/static/config/js/settings.js` (Lakehouse) — `loadDeltaObjects`
   nests an Analytics sub-block inside each domain card, styled like the
   existing Lakebase sync block, with a `work` badge distinguishing scratch
   tables from real output. Unmatched groups render as their own "orphan"
   cards after the domain cards. `dropDeltaDomainObjects` appends the card's
   analytics items to the drop list; the modal boilerplate it shared with the
   new `dropDeltaOrphanObjects` was extracted into `_confirmDropDelta`.
4. `src/front/static/config/js/settings.js` (Lakebase) — Each domain card gains
   a `.lk-analytics-slot` next to its `.lk-sync-slot`, filled by the new
   `loadLakebaseAnalyticsObjects` from the same endpoint. `dropDomainObjects`
   folds the analytics tables into its UC drop pass, and
   `dropLakebaseOrphanObjects` handles the orphan cards. Orphan wiring is
   scoped to `.lk-orphan-card` so the domain cards' own drop handlers are not
   attached twice.
5. `tests/units/settings/test_delta_objects.py` — Added `TestAnalyticsObjectHelpers`
   (suffix and work-table stripping, key agreement and the documented
   divergence, grouping and drop order) and four service cases (matching
   analytics is not an orphan, unmatched analytics is, a configured output
   schema is scanned separately, a failed scan still lists domains).
6. `tests/units/front/test_settings_analytics_objects.py` (new) — Source-contract
   tests over `settings.js`, following the existing front-test pattern: both
   tabs mount their analytics block, both Delete paths include the nested
   tables, both orphan cards exist and are deletable, and the JS match key
   mirrors the server rule. The Lakebase slot is created by JS rather than by a
   template, so there is no markup mount point to assert instead.

## Design notes

- **Nesting under the domain card, not separate top-level cards.** One card per
  domain version is the mental model both tabs already use, and it makes the
  card's Delete the single place that removes everything for that version.
- **`_work_*` tables are listed and deleted with the rest.** They are garbage
  from failed runs; hiding them is how they accumulated in the first place.
- **The match can miss, by design.** `triplestore_<safe>_V<n>` derives `safe`
  from `re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9_]", "_", name.lower())` while the analytics slug comes
  from `sanitize_domain_folder`, which *removes* offending characters rather
  than replacing them. The two agree for an ordinary domain name and diverge for
  a punctuated one. Reconciling the naming rules would touch the build path, so
  the divergence resolves to the orphan card instead — which is exactly the case
  where an operator most needs to find these tables. A unit test pins both
  behaviours.
- **Lakebase orphans come from the server, not from the Postgres cards.** The
  server matches analytics against the UC triple-store objects that every
  engine's Build writes. Matching against the Postgres cards on that tab instead
  would flag every Lakehouse-backed domain's analytics as orphaned.
- **The analytics scan never blanks the tab.** It degrades to an empty list plus
  a warning line, mirroring how `loadLakebaseSyncObjects` degrades to empty
  slots.

